RESEARCH PROGRAMME
Questions across disciplines
The site presents related concepts as a connected research programme while preserving the distinctions between them.
Symbolic Migration
How symbols and cultural forms travel, adapt, and acquire new functions across ritual, literature, and ecological consciousness.
Liminality & Liminal Rhythm
Threshold conditions and the movement through transitional experience — in ritual, music, narrative, and everyday life.
Material Echo Theory
Material environments and architecture as carriers and traces of distributed memory and human presence.
Cultural & Ecological Memory
Relationships among place, environmental change, memory, and identity.
Ritual & Literary Symbolism
Transformations of ritual structures in myth, literature, modernism, and contemporary culture (including the migration of the sacred from Nemi onward).
Environmental Anthropology
How human communities shape, inhabit, and interpret their environments.
AUTHOR’S STATEMENT
I work at the seam between anthropology and literature, where the interpretive habits of one discipline meet the narrative instincts of the other. My concern is with threshold conditions — the rituals, objects, sounds, and landscapes through which people hold memory and make meaning — and with what happens to those forms as they travel.
The paintings extend the same inquiry by other means. Working each portrait in a single colour narrows the likeness to its structure: light, bone, and expression. Film, music, and family are treated alike, so that figures from very different worlds can be read in one visual language.
SELECTED PAINTINGS
Portrait Gallery
Faces from film, music, and family, painted in a single blue register. Reducing each subject to one hue strips the likeness back to structure — light, bone, and expression — and lets figures from very different worlds sit within one visual language.
I
Lee Van Cleef
A low-brimmed hat, a hard sidelong look — the western gunman as a study in stillness and menace. Painted in a single blue register, the portrait reduces the figure to light, shadow, and the weight of a stare.
OIL ON CANVAS · 20 × 16 IN · MONOCHROME BLUE
II
Tim the Enchanter
A bearded enchanter wreathed in flame and halo, caught mid-pronouncement. The baroque swirl of beard and cloak plays against the flat blue ground, turning a comic figure into an icon of prophecy and theatrical dread.
OIL ON CANVAS · 20 × 16 IN · PRIMED CANVAS EDGE VISIBLE
III
Miles Davis
Head bowed, hand pressed to the brow — the trumpeter in a moment of inward concentration. The blue monochrome holds the mood of the late-night session: introspective, close, and unresolved.
OIL ON CANVAS · 20 × 16 IN · GILDED FRAME
IV
Grandma and Grandpa
A double portrait of a couple in mid-century dress, a small pin catching the light at the woman’s shoulder. Family likeness becomes a record of a shared life — the domestic counterpart to the solo portraits.
OIL ON CANVAS · 20 × 16 IN · WALNUT FRAME
V
John Lennon
Long hair, level gaze, the face half-lit and half-swallowed by the dark. A likeness as much of an era as of a person, rendered in the same single-colour discipline as the other portraits.
OIL ON CANVAS · 20 × 16 IN · MONOCHROME BLUE
VI
Lee Marvin
A felt hat, a heavy coat, and a figure standing against a fog-lit ground with a second silhouette rising behind. The composition reads as a frame from a noir — the actor as archetype, the shadow as a second self.
OIL ON CANVAS · 20 × 16 IN · WOOD FRAME
